<article class="env-definition" id="010J">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/010J">Definition <span data-tag="010J">12.6.1</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. Let $A, B \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{A})$. An <em>extension $E$ of $B$ by $A$</em> is a short exact sequence </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to A \to E \to B \to 0.  \]
</div>
  <p> A <em>morphism of extensions</em> between two extensions $0 \to A \to E \to B \to 0$ and $0 \to A \to F \to B \to 0$ means a morphism $f : E \to F$ in $\mathcal{A}$ making the diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ 0 \ar[r] &  A \ar[r] \ar[d]^{\text{id}} &  E \ar[r] \ar[d]^ f &  B \ar[r] \ar[d]^{\text{id}} &  0 \\  0 \ar[r] &  A \ar[r] &  F \ar[r] &  B \ar[r] &  0 }  \]
</div>
  <p> commutative. Thus, the extensions of $B$ by $A$ form a category. </p>
</article>